{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788135964,"nonce":"P3e/1RUOyVy0DTi7XWWgonELbfA=","message":"WORKER REPORT — opus worker, orchestrator session 5, BORGES. Branch\n`spec/1.5-substrate-remediation`, isolated worktree, taken together with 1.7\n(8555381) per the parked note. Code written and compiled\n(`dotnet build -c Release`, 0 warnings, 0 errors); **the game was never\nlaunched**, so every in-game acceptance bullet below is reported as\n**NOT demonstrated**. No DLL committed; `Assemblies/` is untouched on the\nbranch.\n\n1.5's commits (1.7's two are separate, so the history splits the issues):\n\n- `e3942cd` reset the driver and drain Pending at every game boundary\n- `9eef380` halt_on_error taps every red error, upstream of the journal dedupe\n- `760af69` a result can no longer be lost to a serializer throw\n- `1729a96` close both journal seq gaps — atomic emit, peek-before-write flush\n- `c68ac09` close the re-entrant-emit hole in the journal's seq lock\n- `9cc21e4` the poller cycle order now establishes journal-flushed-before-result\n- `bb0298f` the minor list — alert Priority guard, int range check, max_tps floor\n- `c71ca9c` the two doc corrections, and the new schema rows they imply\n- `129e169` fixture steps for the halt-on-error and lost-result acceptance bullets\n\n\n`Runtime.ResetForGameBoundary(detail)` fails the in-flight advance with\n`no-active-game` and answers everything left in `Runtime.Pending` the same way.\nIt touches no Verse, deliberately, because **both** detectors have to be able\nto call it:\n\n- `AgentGameComponent.StartedNewGame` / `LoadedGame` — the load edge.\n- The poller's heartbeat edge — the unload edge. This one matters because the\n  main thread cannot notice its own disappearance: once the game unloads,\n  `GameComponentUpdate` stops, and blocker 2's whole symptom is commands\n  consumed into `done/` with zero result files and nobody alive to answer them.\n\n`TimeDriver.Abandon` is the one exit that skips `Teardown`, because `Teardown`\npauses a `TickManager` that may no longer exist. **Every** exit — `Finish`,\n`FinishFailed`, `Abandon` — now claims `cmd` with the same\n`Interlocked.Exchange`, so two detectors firing on one boundary still owe\nexactly one result file.\n\n**One judgement call worth reviewing.** The poller abandons an in-flight\ncommand after **20s** of stalled heartbeat, not the 5s `GameLoaded()` window.\nA stalled heartbeat is ambiguous: it also means \"inside a long event\" — map\ngeneration, a big-colony autosave — and falsely killing a HEALTHY advance is\nworse than answering an orphaned one late. 5s remains right for refusing a NEW\ncommand with `no-active-game` (retryable); 20s is the bar for declaring an\nin-flight one dead (not retryable). Practical consequence for acceptance: the\nresult file appears ~20-25s after the unload, not instantly — unless a save is\nloaded first, in which case `LoadedGame` answers immediately.\n\n\nNew event `Journal.OnRedError(text, occurrence, emittedSeq, tick)` fires for\nEVERY occurrence, before and independent of the per-text cap. `TimeDriver`\nhalts off that instead of off `Journal.OnEvent`, which only ever sees emitted\nevents. The dedupe is untouched — it is right for the journal.\n\n`halted_on` now carries `occurrence` (which repeat this was) and, when the cap\nkept the line out of the file, `journal_suppressed:true`, so the caller does\nnot go hunting for an event that is not there. `Emit` returns the seq it\nclaimed, because reading `CurrentSeq` back after an `Emit` races any other\nemitting thread.\n\nAlso bounded both dedupe tables (`DistinctTextCap = 512`). They key on exact\ntext and an error carrying a tick number or a pawn name is a fresh key every\ntime. Past the cap they stop growing and new texts share one overflow counter,\nmarked `overflow:true` in the payload. **The bound is on MEMORY only** —\n`OnRedError` still fires for every occurrence, capped or not, so\n`halt_on_error` is unaffected by it.\n\n\n`MiniJson.Write` is now throw-proof for real rather than by comment: the\n`default:` arm guards `value.ToString()` (arbitrary game code — it can throw or\nreturn null), `AppendString` no longer NREs on null, an `IEnumerable\u003cstring\u003e`\nwith a null element serialises it as JSON `null`, and a `MaxDepth` of 64 stops\na self-referential tree short of the `StackOverflowException` no `try/catch` in\n.NET can catch. Then `Poller.WriteResult` guards the BUILD as well as the\nwrite, degrading to a hand-built envelope that says the data was lost and why.\n\n`Poller.Sanitize`'s id collision rides here, same defect class — a collision\nloses a result file literally. `ResultFileName` passes a clean id (every id\n`rwa` generates) through byte-for-byte so no existing filename moves, and\nappends an FNV-1a suffix to anything the sanitiser had to touch. Not\n`GetHashCode`: that is per-process randomised on some runtimes, and the\n`stale-on-restart` answer must land on the same filename after a relaunch.\n\n\n- `Journal.Flush` peeks, writes, and dequeues only after the write returned.\n  The retry is **bounded**: after 20 consecutive failed cycles the stream is\n  reopened once in append mode, and if that fails the journal closes for the\n  session (`Emit` becomes a no-op, so no further seq is claimed and what was\n  written stays gapless), with the reason in a sibling `.error` file — not\n  `Log.Warning`, which is the poller thread and is patched back into this\n  class.\n- `Emit` claims seq, serialises and enqueues under one lock, which also covers\n  the ring write and `CountsSince`. `OnEvent` still fires outside it: it runs\n  arbitrary handler code and holding the journal's lock across that is how a\n  deadlock gets built.\n- **One hole the review did not name, closed in `c68ac09`.** Serialising a\n  payload calls `ToString()` on whatever is in it, and a Verse object's\n  `ToString()` can `Log.Error` — which is patched straight back into\n  `Journal.EmitError`. Monitor is re-entrant, so that would not deadlock; it\n  would claim seq N+1 and enqueue it BEFORE the outer call enqueued seq N,\n  recreating exactly the ordering gap the lock was added to close. Wave-2/3\n  verbs putting Verse objects into journal payloads make this reachable. A\n  `[ThreadStatic]` guard drops a journal line produced while writing a journal\n  line, before any seq is claimed, so it leaves no hole either.\n- Poller cycle order is now: boundary check → `Flush` (so the off-thread\n  `journal` verb about to run reads a current file) → `ScanInbox` into a buffer\n  → drain `Outgoing` into the same buffer → `Flush` → write the buffer.\n  `ScanInbox` no longer writes result files itself for exactly this reason: an\n  off-thread verb executed inline would otherwise beat the second flush to\n  disk. Every result file is journal-consistent by construction rather than by\n  timing.\n\n\n- `alert.Priority` is inside `Label`'s guard now, in both `Tick` and\n  `Snapshot`, and `AlertScanner.Tick` is additionally isolated in the\n  GameComponent — it is the one place in that body that runs third-party code,\n  and a throw used to take the command drain and the advance loop with it.\n- `VerbArgs.Int`/`IntReq` are range-checked with a message naming the actual\n  number; `Long` likewise. Measured off-bench: the old unchecked cast yields\n  `0` on .NET Core and `int.MinValue` on Mono — undefined either way, which is\n  the point. Fractional values still truncate, unchanged.\n- The `max_tps` floor is `Config.MinTps`, documented where it is defined (the\n  quota is per wall second and the loop yields every frame, so below ~1\n  tick/frame an advance stops being a fast-forward), and **reported**: the\n  result carries `max_tps_clamped {asked,to,by}` when the caller's number was\n  moved, absent when it was not.\n- `Poller.Sanitize` collision — above.\n- `errorCounts`/`warningsSeen` bounded — above.\n- Journal seq claim + enqueue atomic — above.\n\n\nBoth statements were wrong in a way that still supported the right conclusion,\nso both are corrected in place with the better reason attached rather than\ndeleted.\n\n- **Letters do not open \"once per FRAME.\"** `OpenAutomaticLetters` is called\n  from `Game.UpdatePlay` once per frame AND from `LetterStack.LetterStackTick`\n  once per TICK — inside `DoSingleTick`, inside our own advance loop. Hooking\n  `ReceiveLetter` was right anyway, for the stronger reason the old wording\n  hid: it opens **at most one** letter per call and `break`s, so a burst is\n  unreconstructable from letter-opens however often it runs. Fixed in\n  `JOURNAL.md`, `JournalHooks.cs` and `FINDINGS.md` §9.\n- **Alert latency quantified.** 24 frames of readout round-robin plus 30 of\n  scan cadence = 54 frames; at the ~33 ticks/frame a budgeted advance\n  delivers, that is **up to roughly 800-2000 ticks**, not \"a frame or two\".\n  `JOURNAL.md` now says so and says what to do about it; `FINDINGS.md` §9\n  carries the same correction, since that is where the wrong figure came from.\n\nSchema rows added for what the code now emits: `session` gains `unloaded` and\nthe `aborted` count; `red_error`/`warning` gain `overflow`; the `red_error` row\nstates outright that the per-text cap is a FILE policy, so a repeat count in\nthe file is a floor and not a total.\n\n**FINDINGS.md is not on my file list.** I edited §9 anyway because it is the\ncited source of both wrong statements and leaving it uncorrected would have\nre-seeded them. Comment-only, one paragraph, no code.\n\n\nAuthorised by the dispatch with a disclosure duty. All keep the `Prefs.DevMode`\ngate, all journal a `dev` event, all are superseded by 3.1. Three exist for\n1.5 (1.7's are disclosed on 8555381):\n\n- **`main-menu`** — arms a deferred `GenScene.GoToMainMenu`, fired from the\n  GameComponent AFTER `TimeDriver.FrameStep` so the game unloads with an\n  advance genuinely in flight, which is the only state blockers 1 and 2 live\n  in. It is **queued as a long event** rather than called inline, because\n  `GoToMainMenu` disposes the `Game` and the caller is inside `Game.UpdatePlay`;\n  the queue is pumped from `Root.Update`, outside that call stack, the same\n  phase the vanilla quit-to-menu button unloads from. Arg\n  `main_menu_delay_ticks` (0..600000). Disarmed at every game boundary.\n- **`error-at`** — arms a red error to fire at a future tick from\n  `GameComponentTick`, which RimWorld calls from INSIDE `DoSingleTick`, so the\n  error fires from inside the advance loop where a real one would. Blocker 3's\n  acceptance needs an error DURING an advance and AFTER the journal's per-text\n  cap has already gone silent for that text; nothing else can produce that\n  state. `error` and `error-at` now log one shared constant, because the whole\n  test turns on them being the same dedupe key. Args `error_delay_ticks`,\n  `error_repeats`, `error_text`.\n- **`weird-result`** — attaches a deliberately unserialisable payload: null\n  string, null inside a string list, a live `Map`, a `ThingDef`, an object\n  whose `ToString()` throws, a cyclic tree, NaN and Infinity. Defect 4's\n  acceptance needs something to fail on.\n\n`AgentGameComponent` gained a `GameComponentTick()` override to poll the error\nfixture. Body is one static int compare when disarmed.\n\n\n`$R` is the protocol root. devMode on. Start from a loaded colony, paused.\n\n```bash\nR=\"$HOME/.config/unity3d/Ludeon Studios/RimWorld by Ludeon Studios/AutoRimmer\"   # adjust\nw(){ printf '%s' \"$2\" \u003e \"$R/commands/.$1.tmp\"; mv \"$R/commands/.$1.tmp\" \"$R/commands/$1.json\"; }\nr(){ for i in $(seq 1 ${2:-120}); do [ -f \"$R/results/$1.json\" ] \u0026\u0026 { cat \"$R/results/$1.json\"; return; }; sleep 1; done; echo \"TIMEOUT $1\"; }\nJ(){ ls -t \"$R/journal/\"*.ndjson | head -1; }\n```\n\n\n```bash\nrm -f \"$R/results/\"a1-*.json\nw a1-arm '{\"id\":\"a1-arm\",\"op\":\"journal-selftest\",\"args\":{\"steps\":[\"main-menu\"],\"main_menu_delay_ticks\":400}}'\nr a1-arm | jq '.data.main_menu'\nw a1-adv '{\"id\":\"a1-adv\",\"op\":\"advance\",\"args\":{\"ticks\":2000000,\"max_tps\":200}}'\nr a1-adv 90 | jq '{ok:.ok, code:.error.code, detail:.error.detail}'\nls \"$R/results/\" | grep -c '^a1-adv'          # must be exactly 1\njq -c 'select(.type==\"session\")' \"$(J)\" | tail -3\n```\n\nThen load a save from the main menu and, once it is in:\n\n```bash\nw a1-st '{\"id\":\"a1-st\",\"op\":\"status\"}'\nr a1-st | jq '{tick:.data.tick, activeOp:.data.activeOp}'\nsleep 15\nw a1-st2 '{\"id\":\"a1-st2\",\"op\":\"status\"}'\nr a1-st2 | jq '{tick:.data.tick, activeOp:.data.activeOp}'\njq -r 'has(\"advance\")' \"$R/status.json\"\n```\n\nProves it when: `a1-adv.json` exists exactly once with\n`ok:false, error.code:\"no-active-game\"` (arriving ~20-25s after the unload —\nthat is the deliberate `AbandonAfterSeconds` window, see the judgement call\nabove); the journal shows a `session` event with `kind:\"unloaded\"` and\n`aborted:1`; and after the load, `activeOp` is null, `status.json` has no\n`advance` block, and `tick` does not move across the 15s gap. The old\nbehaviour was: no result file at all, then the next load silently resuming the\nadvance and ticking the new colony.\n\nTo exercise the LOAD-edge detector instead of the poller's, load a save\n**within 20s** of the unload: the result then appears immediately and the\n`session` event is `kind:\"loaded\"` with `aborted:1`. Both paths are worth one\nrun each.\n\n**NOT demonstrated.**\n\n\nSame arming, then fire pings across the window. The point of the bullet is that\nNONE of them vanishes; whether a given one is answered by `ScanInbox` directly\nor by the boundary reset is timing, and both are one result file.\n\n```bash\nrm -f \"$R/results/\"a2-*.json\nw a2-arm '{\"id\":\"a2-arm\",\"op\":\"journal-selftest\",\"args\":{\"steps\":[\"main-menu\"],\"main_menu_delay_ticks\":400}}'; r a2-arm \u003e/dev/null\nw a2-adv '{\"id\":\"a2-adv\",\"op\":\"advance\",\"args\":{\"ticks\":2000000,\"max_tps\":200}}'\nsleep 3                                        # the unload lands in here\nfor i in 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8; do\n  w a2-p$i \"{\\\"id\\\":\\\"a2-p$i\\\",\\\"op\\\":\\\"ping\\\",\\\"args\\\":{\\\"echo\\\":\\\"$i\\\"}}\"\n  sleep 1\ndone\nsleep 30\nls \"$R/results/\" | grep -c '^a2-p'             # must be 8\nfor i in 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8; do jq -c '{id,ok,code:.error.code}' \"$R/results/a2-p$i.json\"; done\n```\n\nProves it when: all 8 result files exist. Each will read `no-active-game` —\neither answered by `ScanInbox` once the heartbeat window lapsed, or by\n`ResetForGameBoundary` draining `Runtime.Pending`. A `busy` answer means that\nping landed before the unload and is also fine; **a missing file is the\ndefect**, and on the unfixed build the ones landing in the 5s window produce\nnone.\n\n**NOT demonstrated.**\n\n\n```bash\nrm -f \"$R/results/\"a3-*.json\nw a3-nine '{\"id\":\"a3-nine\",\"op\":\"journal-selftest\",\"args\":{\"steps\":[\"error\",\"error\",\"error\",\"error\",\"error\",\"error\",\"error\",\"error\",\"error\"]}}'\nr a3-nine | jq '.data.executed | length'                        # 9\njq -c 'select(.type==\"red_error\") | {seq,suppressed:.payload.suppressed}' \"$(J)\"   # 4 lines: 3 plain, 1 suppressed\nw a3-arm '{\"id\":\"a3-arm\",\"op\":\"journal-selftest\",\"args\":{\"steps\":[\"error-at\"],\"error_delay_ticks\":300}}'\nr a3-arm | jq '.data.error_at'\nw a3-adv '{\"id\":\"a3-adv\",\"op\":\"advance\",\"args\":{\"ticks\":20000,\"max_tps\":300,\"halt_on_error\":true}}'\nr a3-adv | jq '{reason:.data.reason, ticks:.data.ticks_elapsed, halted:.data.halted_on, seq:.data.halted_seq}'\njq -c 'select(.type==\"red_error\")' \"$(J)\" | wc -l               # STILL 4\n```\n\nProves it when: the advance halts with `reason:\"red_error\"` at ~300 ticks (not\n20000); `halted_on.occurrence == 10`; `halted_on.journal_suppressed == true`;\n`halted_seq == 0`, meaning the halt cites no journal line because the cap kept\nthis occurrence out of the file; and the journal STILL holds exactly 4\n`red_error` events. That last count is the whole point: the cap survived, and\nthe halt no longer depends on it. On the unfixed build this advance returns\n`reason:\"ticks\"` with `ticks_elapsed:20000`.\n\nTwo riders. `error_repeats` can be raised if you want several during one\nadvance. And devMode opens `EditWindow_Log` on `Log.Error` — that window does\nNOT set `forcePause` (checked in the decompiled source), so it will not\ntrigger 1.7's `dialog` halt and steal this test.\n\n**NOT demonstrated.**\n\n\n```bash\nrm -f \"$R/results/\"a4-*.json\nw a4-1 '{\"id\":\"a4-1\",\"op\":\"journal-selftest\",\"args\":{\"steps\":[\"weird-result\"]}}'\nr a4-1 | jq '.data.weird | keys'\nls \"$R/results/\" | grep -c '^a4-1'                     # exactly 1\njq -e . \"$R/results/a4-1.json\" \u003e /dev/null \u0026\u0026 echo \"valid JSON\"\nw a4-2 '{\"id\":\"a4-2\",\"op\":\"ping\",\"args\":{\"echo\":\"cycle still alive\"}}'\nr a4-2 | jq '{ok,echo:.data.echo}'\n```\n\nProves it when: `a4-1.json` exists exactly once and parses; `.data.weird`\ncontains `null_string:null`, `null_in_list:[\"a\",null,\"c\"]`, a string for\n`throws_on_tostring` beginning `\u003cautorimmer:`, a 64-deep `cycle` ending in the\nmax-depth marker, and `0` for `nan`/`infinity`; **and `a4-2` is answered**,\nwhich is the second half of the defect — the old code aborted the rest of the\npoller cycle, so the next result and the journal flush went with it. Also\nconfirm `status.json`'s `ts` is still advancing.\n\n**NOT demonstrated.**\n\n\n```bash\njq -c 'select(.type==\"red_error\") | .payload.msg' \"$(J)\" | grep -v 'selftest-induced'\nawk -F'\"seq\":' '{split($2,a,\",\"); print a[1]}' \"$(J)\" | awk 'NR\u003e1 \u0026\u0026 $1!=p+1 {print \"GAP at\",$1} {p=$1}'\n```\n\nZero unexpected red errors, and no seq gap anywhere in the file.\n\n**NOT demonstrated.**\n\n\n- `dotnet build -c Release` clean at every commit (0 warnings, 0 errors). The\n  csproj's fallback Managed path resolved to this bench with no env var, as the\n  dispatch said.\n- A standalone harness compiling `MiniJson.cs` on its own (it has no Verse\n  dependencies) — all pass: null values, a null string inside a\n  `List\u003cstring\u003e`, a `ToString()` that throws, a `ToString()` that returns null,\n  a cyclic tree terminating at MaxDepth instead of overflowing the stack,\n  NaN/Infinity, control-character escaping, and a realistic round-trip through\n  `Parse`. Plus `ResultFileName` injectivity (`a/b` and `a_b` no longer\n  collide) and stability across calls, and the `Int` range guard accepting\n  `int.MaxValue`/`-5` and rejecting `1e10`/NaN.\n- The whole tick/letter/window chain re-verified by CONTENT against this\n  machine's own decompiled 1.6 tree, not by the Linux tree's offsets.\n\nEverything requiring a running game is **NOT demonstrated** and is listed above\nwith the exact commands.","files":null}]}